INT. FUNERAL HOME - FAMILY ROOM - DAY

Beige carpet, a box of tissues on every surface. A framed print of a wheat field. A coffee urn steams on a side table.

OLIVER STANHOPE, 50s, in a black suit too big at the shoulders, stands facing the wall. His sister, WINIFRED STANHOPE-DUVAL, 50s, sits on the sofa with a binder of arrangements.

WINIFRED
We should choose the casket today.

OLIVER
Dad wanted pine.

WINIFRED
Dad wanted a lot of things.

OLIVER
He wanted pine.

WINIFRED
He wanted to be left alone.

The door opens. LUCIAN STANHOPE, 20s, their nephew, steps in, hair wet from rain, a duffel on his shoulder.

LUCIAN
I got the earlier flight.

OLIVER
You weren't invited.

WINIFRED
Oliver.

LUCIAN
It's my grandfather.

OLIVER
It's my father.

WINIFRED
Both of you stop.

LUCIAN
I didn't come to fight.

OLIVER
You came to take the watch.

LUCIAN
I came to say goodbye!

WINIFRED
Quiet! Both of you, quiet!

Oliver swings around. His hand shakes. The binder slides off Winifred's lap and hits the floor with a slap. Nobody picks it up.
